  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving public health in the Niger through Japanese innovation in waste management, disease screening and electrification</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project improves public health conditions for host communities, migrants, refugees, and internally displaced persons in Niger by integrating Japanese technology and innovations adapted to local contexts in disease screening, electrification and waste management. Interventions will enhance TB and HIV screening capacity along migratory routes in the region. In Awaridi, Diffa region, the project will establish a clean water supply system and electrify the Integrated Health Centre (CSI) and local households using a solar energy system. The initiative will also empower women and people with disabilities to actively participate in decision-making, activity implementation, and beneficiary selection throughout all activities in the communities. In the Agadez region, the project will leverage the expertise of the Research Institute for Humanity and Nature at Kyoto University to address waste management challenges and rehabilitate degraded land for pasturage and farming. The Ministry of Environment will enhance its capacity to analyse environmental quality by installing testing equipment. The project will increase business opportunities for youth and women, reduce conflicts related to pasture and farming by collaborating with other initiatives in the area, and support local educational institutions and public health schools to conduct research on environmental issues.</narrative>
  </description>
